+# Summary: range matching (`satisfies`, `max_satisfying`)
+
+## What changed
+
+- `depres/ranges.py`: implemented `satisfies(version, range_)` and `max_satisfying(versions, range_)` per `docs/RANGES.md`.
+  - Ranges are parsed into comparator sets joined by `||`. Each comparator expands into atoms (`>=`, `<`, `==`, ...) using the doc's rules for partial versions, X-ranges, hyphen, tilde and caret.
+  - The prerelease rule is applied per set: a prerelease version matches only if some bound in the same set names the same `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` with a prerelease tag.
+  - Empty sets and `*` match every release version and no prerelease.
+  - Invalid ranges and invalid versions raise `ValueError`.
+- `tests/test_ranges_contract.py` (new): 23 added tests covering the doc's tables and rules. No existing test was edited or deleted.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `python -m unittest -v` in the project root: 23 tests run, all pass, including the 8 in `tests/test_ranges.py` that previously errored.
+
+## Not verified / decisions to check
+
+- No CI config file exists in this repo, so I could not confirm what CI runs beyond `python -m unittest`. No linter or type checker was run.
+- Choices the doc does not settle, which I made myself:
+  - A leading `v` is accepted in range comparators (e.g. `>=v1.2.3`), not only in versions.
+  - Partial versions with a prerelease tag (e.g. `1.2-beta`) are rejected as invalid.
+  - `max_satisfying` returns the first item when several items compare equal (for example `1.0.0+a` and `1.0.0+b`).
+  - `<x` and `>*` are treated as matching nothing.
+- `depres/__init__.py` already exports both functions, so no change was needed there.
